Subject: [PATCH] check the wiki's setting defaults against the source

wiki/Settings.md printed eleven things Settings.java does not say. Seven defaults
were wrong - primaryTimeoutMS 2000 against 500, failureTimeoutMS 5000 against 2000,
planAheadPrimaryTimeoutMS 1500 against 4000, planAheadFailureTimeoutMS 3500 against
5000, pathingMaxChunkBorderFetch 16 against 50, allowDiagonalAscend true against
false, and movementTimeoutTicks 10 against 100. Two settings were named that the
source does not declare, buildRepeatSpacing and elytraSwapMinimumDurability (the
real name is elytraMinimumDurability). buildRepeat was printed as a boolean where it
is a Vec3i offset. acceptableThrowawayItems was missing stone from its four items.

A reader types these into chat and sets these numbers, so a wrong row is not a
documentation nit. The worst is movementTimeoutTicks: "restoring the documented
default" would give every movement a tenth of its time and then look like a bug in
the pather. Nothing caught any of it, because the page was prose and the source was
Java and nothing compared them.

SettingsWikiTest compares them. It reads the wiki's rows and asserts, against
src/api/java/baritone/api/Settings.java, that the setting exists and that the
printed default is the declared one. The price is that defaults are written the way
the source writes them - 500 for 500L, 0,0,0 for new Vec3i(0, 0, 0), block names for
an item list - and that a default in a shape the test does not know fails the build
asking to be taught rather than being skipped quietly.

Proven against the real bug rather than a synthetic one: run against the page as it
was, the test fails two of three and prints exactly the eleven - the two invented
names from the name test and the nine wrong defaults from the value test, each as
"the wiki says X, the source says Y".

The declaration pattern is held to the file as well. It first parsed 246 of the 248
settings, missing buildSubstitutes and buildValidSubstitutes, which are
Setting<Map<Block, List<Block>>> - two levels of nesting where the pattern allowed
one. Neither is on the wiki today, so the suite stayed green while the test was one
documented setting away from calling a real one invented, which is the mistake it
exists to catch in the other direction. The pattern now covers three levels and
parses 248 of 248, and declaredDefaults counts what it parsed against the number of
times "public final Setting<" is spelled in the file, failing if they differ. A
declaration nested four deep trips it with the cause named.

wiki/Settings.md gains the source-of-truth note, mirroring the one on
Humanlike-Features.md. Settings.java is not touched. 46 classes / 376 tests /
0 failures / 0 errors / 0 skipped, up from 45/373.
